//! The front-end path per frequency, every path compared: 97 grid
//! frequencies from 42 to 1002 MHz, each (RFFILT, RFMUX) at TF 0000/0000,
//! in 3 runs (5238 path readings; 0 writes retried after a stall), and the
//! segments of neighbours sharing the path the driver takes. The driver
//! takes its path from `BAND_PATH_SEGMENTS` (src/band.rs).
//!
//! Method (findings.md step 4f2, harness `band-paths`): at each frequency
//! the LO was programmed (IF 4.57 MHz above the RF), the source re-levelled
//! once at the bypass path (RFFILT 00, RFMUX 01) and then held — it stayed
//! at +10 dB relative at every frequency of every run — and the bypass, the
//! 16 paths and the bypass again were each written, left 300 ms, and the
//! meter (`if_agc_val`, MSB-first, vagc configuration) averaged over 700 ms
//! of back-to-back reads. `path-settle` (findings.md step 4f2) found a path
//! change's transient over well inside that wait and the meter wandering
//! about 1 dB on its own; read through the same window, its transitions
//! repeat within seconds to 0.26 dB at worst (standard deviation, over 11
//! frequency-path groups). Across the runs, taken minutes to hours apart, a
//! path's reading repeats to 0.18 dB (standard deviation pooled over 487
//! frequency-path cells; the worst cell 0.65 dB). Readings are in dB
//! relative to the mean of the frequency's two bypass readings (207.2
//! counts per dB, positive = more signal); a path whose mean sat within 98%
//! of the rail passed nothing.
//!
//! The path with the most signal is decided by each path's mean over the
//! runs. A margin under `TIE_DB` (2 times the runs' pooled standard
//! deviation) is a tie; where the most-signal path ties with the dominant
//! path (10, 01) — the most signal at 93 of 97 frequencies — and the runs
//! did not all pick it on their own, the dominant path is kept
//! (`tie_kept_dominant`), so the table does not follow noise from one grid
//! point to the next. A path every run picked is kept even inside a tie:
//! were the two equal, each run would favour either with even odds.
//!
//! Results: the runs' own best paths agree at 94 of 97 frequencies; the
//! best path beats the next by a median 1.76 dB (under 0.35 dB, a tie, at
//! 5); the dominant path is kept over a tying one at 1; the best path's
//! reading differs between runs by a median 0.18 dB, at most 1.29 dB; the
//! bypass drifted across a frequency's sequence by a median 0.12 dB, at
//! most 0.87 dB.
//!
//! Caveats: a single tone measures each path's passband gain at the tone,
//! not its rejection of anything else; the corner codes (TF_NCH, TF_LP) are
//! left at 0000/0000, the least-attenuating, and not measured here; the
//! grid is 10 MHz, so a segment boundary lies somewhere between two grid
//! points.

